<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leo I, King of Armenia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1150–1219</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Levon the Magnificent, Leo II of Armenia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leo I, called the Magnificent, was the Rubenid prince of Armenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> from 1187 (as Leo II) and its first king from 1198 to 1219. He secured a crown from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> Henry VI and Pope Celestine III and was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a>, turning the principality into a kingdom. He adopted Frankish feudal institutions, encouraged Italian trade and fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> over its succession.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leo-i-king-of-armenia.jpg" alt="Leo I, King of Armenia" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cilician Armenia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Levon_I_coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
